## FORMAT A: Coded Building IDs (B###/R#### Patterns)

### Building ID Detection
- Format: `B##A` or `B###` (e.g., B00A, B00B, B001, B009, B010)
- Typically followed by dash and building name: `B00A - Admin Building`
- May include year and construction: `B00A - Admin Building - 1924 - Brick`
- Demountable buildings: `D##` (e.g., D01, D02, D03)

### Room Detection
- Format: `{BuildingID}-R####` (e.g., B00A-R0001, B009-R0005)
- Count rooms per building to inform complexity classification

---

## FORMAT B: Named Buildings (No Coded IDs)

### Building Detection
Some documents identify buildings by **name** rather than codes:
```
Building Name:   Main Building
```
or building names appear as section headers, table group headers, or repeated page headers.

Each building typically starts on a new page with metadata such as:
- Building name
- Number of Levels
- Construction Type
- Address

### Section Dividers
Buildings may have section dividers for Interior/Exterior areas:
```
Main Building - Exterior - Ground Level
Main Building - Interior - Ground Level
```
These are NOT separate buildings - they are areas within the same building.

### Room Detection
When no coded room IDs exist, rooms are identified by name:
- "Boiler Room", "Ward 12", "Kitchen", "External - Throughout"
- Count distinct room names per building for complexity estimation

### When No Building Codes Exist
If the document does not use coded building identifiers (B###, D## etc.):
- Generate sequential IDs: B001, B002, B003, etc.
- Set `building_id` to the generated code AND `name` to the actual building name
- Example: building_id = "B001", name = "Main Building"
- NEVER use the building name as the building_id — always generate a short code

